"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 9.3. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), pT2 stage, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymph node metastasis. The biopsy showed a higher grade (Gleason 4+3, ISUP grade group 3). The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding.\n\nCountervailing factors include the positive surgical margins and the higher grade identified on biopsy compared to the resection specimen. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory. The nodal status was documented as negative, which is reassuring. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trend is crucial for refining the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 46.88 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.51–56.26 months; its coverage has not been validated."
